// Why a filesystem operation did not happen.
//
// The shipped shape is ten integers and a string, carried in two structs:
//
//     export struct Change { i32 fault; string message; bool ok(const this) { … } }
//     export struct FileResult { bool ok; u8[] bytes; string error; i32 fault; }
//
// with `FAULT_NONE`, `FAULT_DENIED`, `FAULT_EXISTS`, `FAULT_NOT_EMPTY`, `FAULT_NOT_FOUND`,
// `FAULT_IS_DIR`, `FAULT_NOT_A_DIR`, `FAULT_READ_ONLY`, `FAULT_UNSUPPORTED` and `FAULT_OTHER` as
// functions returning small integers.
//
// **Worth putting beside `vision/packages/tor`, because the two are opposite failures of the same
// gap.** `tor` has 64 refusals collapsing into 13 booleans and one reason in 16,291 lines: nobody
// could afford to say *why*. `fs` says why ten different ways and cannot make the compiler check any
// of it — `FAULT_NONE` is a valid value of the same type as `FAULT_DENIED`, so a caller may compare
// against the wrong one, forget to compare at all, or read `bytes` from a result whose `ok` is
// false. One package gave up on reasons and the other has reasons a type cannot hold.
//
// A union costs neither. The members below are the ten codes with the payload each of them actually
// wants, which is the thing an integer cannot carry: `NotFound` wants the path, `Denied` wants the
// path and what was attempted, `Other` wants the host's own words — which is what the separate
// `string message` field beside the integer has always been for.

import {
  NotGranted, NotFound, Denied, Exists, IsDir, NotADir, NotEmpty, ReadOnly, Unsupported, OtherFault,
} from "std";

/*
 * **These nine were declared here until 2026-09-05 and are `std`'s now.**
 *
 * They were written from the shipped `FAULT_*` codes, and at the time `std`'s `Files` answered
 * `Result<…, NotGranted>` — one bit — so a filesystem taxonomy had nowhere else to live. Then the
 * audit found that `std` had *dropped* the shipped `i32 fault` when it turned `FileResult` into a
 * `Result`, and putting it back means `Files.read` answers a `FileFault` with these nine members in
 * it.
 *
 * At which point they were declared twice, in two files, for one taxonomy — the drift this directory
 * has found four times elsewhere, created here by a fix. They live in `std` because the layering
 * rule says so: **a value that appears in a capability's signature must be declared at or below the
 * capability layer**, and they now appear in eight of them.
 *
 * What stays here is the `Fault` union, because *which* of them a mount can produce is this
 * package's business and not `std`'s — a memory mount produces all nine and a host mount produces
 * whatever the host reports.
 *
 * **And that last sentence is aspirational, which was checked the next morning.** `Mount` is a
 * struct of funcrefs with **one** error type in every slot, so `inMemory()` and `onHost()` both
 * answer this union and a per-implementation one has nowhere to go. The funcref struct is chosen
 * deliberately — *"a method would dispatch on `Mount`'s own type and there is only one of those"* —
 * so the decision that makes mounts composable is the same decision that forbids per-implementation
 * error sets.
 *
 * `Fault` is therefore the **superset**, and the cost is a caller matching on it must handle members
 * its mount can never produce. `Mount<E>` would fix it and would make a filesystem holding both
 * kinds impossible to write, since the two mounts would be different types. `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.
 */

export union<
  NotGranted, NotFound, Denied, Exists, IsDir, NotADir, NotEmpty, ReadOnly, Unsupported, OtherFault
> Fault;

/**
 * And the members, re-exported — **importing a vocabulary does not publish it.**
 *
 * This file imports the nine from `"std"` and declares the union over them, so it exported a type
 * whose arms were not reachable through it: `./fs.wac` asked for them and got nothing. That is
 * `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *24 of 29 barrels export an enum or union without its arms* one level
 * down, at a **file** rather than a barrel, and the mechanism is different in a way worth keeping:
 * a barrel omits arms by forgetting them, and this omitted them because **it never had them to
 * export** — they arrived by `import` and an import is not an export.
 *
 * A caller of `@/packages/fs` should not have to know that this package's vocabulary is `std`'s.
 */
export {
  NotGranted, NotFound, Denied, Exists, IsDir, NotADir, NotEmpty, ReadOnly, Unsupported, OtherFault
} from "std";

/**
 * There is no `None`.
 *
 * `FAULT_NONE` is the tenth constant and it is the one that does not belong: it names the *absence*
 * of a fault and shares a type with every fault, which is what makes `if (c.fault == FAULT_NONE())`
 * the shape of every caller and `if (c.fault == FAULT_DENIED())` — with the sense inverted by
 * accident — a bug nothing catches. In a `Result` the absence is `Ok` and is a different arm of a
 * different type, so the comparison cannot be written at all.
 *
 * That is the whole of what the union buys over the integers, and it is worth writing down plainly
 * because it is not "unions are nicer": it is one specific value that exists only to be compared
 * against, and it stops existing.
 */
